tube torsion\n\nIn this section we focus on a speciﬁc version of Legendrian higher torsion which we call Legendrian\n\ntube torsion.\n\n3.1. Generating functions for nearby Lagrangians. We recall that a nearby Lagrangian is an ex-\n\nact Lagrangian submanifold L⊂T∗M, where L and M smooth manifolds which are closed, connected\n\nand of the same dimension. The nearby Lagrangian conjecture states that any nearby Lagrangian is\n\nisotopic through nearby Lagrangians to the zero section.\n\nFor a nearby Lagrangian L⊂T∗M to admit a generating function on a ﬁber bundle F→W→M, it\n\nis necessary that the ﬁberwise Morse homology of the generating function have Euler characteristic ±1.\n\nIndeed, the projection L→M is known to be a homotopy equivalence [FSS, Na09, A12, Kra13, Gui12],\n\nRemark 4.7.To be precise, this means that the doubling construction of [ACGK] produces a generating\n\nfunction g for L which is of tube type in the sense of [ACGK] and such that via the modiﬁcation of\n\n[AACK] the function g corresponds to f. So f is a ﬁbered function of tube type in the sense of the\n\npresent article. Note that f being natural implies, but is not a priori equivalent to, that the tube\n\nbundle W=T(f) is in the natural coset of the rigid tube map.\n\n28 DANIEL ´\n\nALVAREZ-GAVELA, KIYOSHI IGUSA, AND MIKE SULLIVAN\n\nRemark 4.8.For i= 0,1 let L i⊂T∗M be a nearby Lagrangian and let Λ i⊂J 1 M be corresponding\n\nLegendrian lifts. It could in principle be possible that L 0 and L 1 are not Hamiltonian isotopic but Λ 0\n\nand Λ 1 are Legendrian isotopic. If so, then τ(Λ 0)=τ(Λ 1), i.e. their tube torsions agree. However the\n\nauthors do not know whether tube torsion need be a single p-coset, in principle it could be larger. In\n\nparticular it could be the case that ν(L 0)=ν(L 1), i.e. that the nearby Lagrangian torsion distinguished\nparametrized Morse theory of f.\n\n30 DANIEL ´\n\nALVAREZ-GAVELA, KIYOSHI IGUSA, AND MIKE SULLIVAN\n\nLet us put M=S n for concreteness, and note 